            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

As discussed, your house has some significant air leakage that is coming from a few key areas:



** The chimney of the current furnace, where it has been taken apart at the top of the furnace ....... essentially creating an opening (equivalent to a 6.25" diameter pipe) up &amp; out of your house



** The chimney and combustion air pipe inside the hot water tank room (as long as the lower access door into that room is open)



** The attic hatch has leakage around the opening and you could seal it with a removable caulking such as "Draft Attack" 



** We had identified ALOT of air leakage coming in from where the 2 halves of your "double wide" manufactured house are adjoined. We could feel &amp; see (with the Infrared Camera) cold air coming up from the crawlspace below (through door frames, floor trim, wall plugs &amp; switches) and also the upper wall adjacent to the entrance to the master bathroom. Ideally, the lower air leakage would be best sealed from below (in the crawlspace) perhaps with spray foam insulation. The upper leakage is best sealed from inside the attic (again with spray foam) however the master bathroom is a long way from where the attic hatch is ........ so a lower "strategic" priority.



Savings: I would expect you should achieve about a 40% reduction in air leakage, and you could potentially save $ 746 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 19.6 GJ. There are no rebates for reducing air leakage, unless a homeowner is also completing an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), which I don't think is an upgrade you are considering as you plan to move to Alberta in 2021. However, just the annual energy savings alone represent a GREAT payback for your air sealing efforts !!</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral>

The cathedral ceiling of your house would appear to have R24 batt insulation. In conjunction with your extensive renovation plans, I have modeled the opportunity to strap the ceiling down another 1.5" and replace the existing insulation with R40 ......... either batt insulation or spray foam - whatever you choose.



Savings: By increasing the ceiling insulation from R24 up to a minimum R40, you could potentially save $ 170 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 500 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program</ceilingsCathedral>
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 151 litre gas-fired hot water tank (with a continuous pilot light), that vents up a 3" chimney &amp; the "enclosed" room it is located in has a 3" combustion air supply pipe coming in through the floor from the crawlspace below. There is a small access door in the bottom of the hot water tank room cover panel &amp; that small access door was open upon my arrival (and it sounds like it is usually that way). With that door being open, this creates the opportunity for the 3" chimney for the current hot water tank to continuously draw inside heated air up &amp; out of your house.



I have modeled the opportunity to replace the hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 160 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 750 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP). Please note these savings only represent the higher efficiency of the new hot water heater. The savings associated with eliminating the 3" chimney &amp; the 3" combustion air supply pipe are combined in with the furnace upgrade savings in the previous recommendation.

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your house is currently heated with an older 80% efficient gas furnace. This furnace is designed to have a direct vent, concentric "combination" chimney flue pipe (in the centre) and an outer dedicated combustion air pipe. I believe the diameter of the inner flue pipe is 5" and the exterior combustion air pipe is 8" and we identified that the outer combustion air pipe had been raised up away from the furnace .......... so that the direct vent design of this furnace no longer exists.



The area of the 8" pipe (less the inner 5" pipe) is equivalent to a 6.25" diameter pipe that is continuously open up &amp; out of your house. This volume of heated air is therefore continuously leaving your house all winter long - whether the furnace is operating of not. In addition, the 5" centre flue pipe will also be drawing warm interior air up &amp; out of the house



I have modeled an upgrade from this older furnace (including the way the current chimney is set up) to an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified high-efficiency condensing gas furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. The new furnace will no longer require the existing chimney &amp; can be direct vented out the back or side wall of the house, using 2 x PVC pipes.



Savings: By replacing your current furnace with a 95% AFUE high-efficiency condensing gas furnace, that incorporates an ECM fan blower (and eliminating the current chimney heat loss), you could potentially save $ 1,128 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 28.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,500 in rebates from the Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

WoodStove Heat



We talked about using your woodstove for heating the house and how you can use your furnace fan to circulate that heat throughout the house. If you choose to upgrade to a new 95% efficient furnace (modeled previously in this report) with the high efficiency ECM blower motor, the new ECM motor would be an efficient way to circulate the wood heat. Many homeowners will have their heating contractors install a 2nd thermosat that will operate in "Pretend AC" mode. When the thermostat reads an upper limit number (eg: 25C) created by the wood stove, it sends a signal to the furnace fan to come on to circulate the heat. When the woodstove is off, the regular thermostat has your programmed lower limit number (eg: 20C) for the furnace to come on &amp; heat the house.



Using our current Inuvik fuel cost library data for wood (see the Fuel Cost data 4 sections below), I ran a quick calculation to see how much you would save (compared to the new furnace upgrade) if you used your wood stove for 50% of the annual heating of your home.



1. If you are buying wood at $472.50 per cord, the supplementary annual savings are only $220 per year

2. If you are using wood at our deemed $100.00 per cord "self-harvested" value (amortized cost per cord of a chainsaw, gas/oil, chain sharpening, safety gear, truck &amp; gas to haul the wood, etc.), the supplementary annual savings are $1,856 per year



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Inuvik, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 400 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the range hood, both bathroom fans &amp; the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.9 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not expected to cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. The current furnace and hot water tank are supposed to be set up as "direct vent" appliances, however the chimney on the furnace has been partially taken apart &amp; the lower access panel to the hot water tank closet is in was in the "open" position......... so both of these appliances can be susceptible to having combustion products back-drafted into the house. The woodstove has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe connected, so it is less susceptible to back drafting (as long as the door is closed).



During my assessment of your home, I did see smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors, however the smoke detectors appeared to be older, outdated models. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following ventilation:

        - Kitchen range hood exhaust fan

        - 2 bathroom exhaust fans

        - Clothes dryer



Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Inuvik



The following Nov. 1, 2019 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Inuvik:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.32 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.73 per kWh thereafter

Synthetic Natural Gas (Utilidor Piped) - $37.21/Gigajoule

Oil  - $1.46 per litre 

Propane - $0.69 per litre delivered

Wood - $472.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $12.60 per bag, $630.00 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



